    "html": "<h2>August 23 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment (Increment 02)</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, serial multi-part narrative structure, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 23</strong> across American network and military broadcasting history (1932–1955):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 23 Archival Inventory (Increment 02)</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Eno_Crime_Clues_1932_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>From This Dark Stairway PT 1</code>, <code>Spencer Dean Serial</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;From This Dark Stairway (Part 1) — Spencer Dean Mystery&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Blue (WJZ)</strong><br>1932-08-23<br>9:30 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>J.C. Eno Ltd.</strong><br>Eno Effervescent Fruit Salt</td>\n<td><strong>Edward Reese</strong> (Spencer Dean), <strong>John Kane</strong> (Dan Cassidy), <strong>Walter Connolly</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mignon G. Eberhart</strong> (Author), <strong>Stewart Sterling</strong> (Adapter), <strong>William S. Rainey</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Hardboiled&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;, &quot;Serial&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In Part 1 of this multi-part serial mystery adapted from Mignon G. Eberhart, Spencer Dean (The Manhunter) and sidekick Dan Cassidy investigate the midnight stabbing murder of a prominent surgeon in a dark hospital service stairway, uncovering a web of blackmail among the surgical staff.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Glenn_Miller_1939_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Glen Island Casino</code>, <code>Glenn Miller Chesterfield 1939</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Glenn Miller Orchestra Live at Glen Island Casino (Chesterfield Moonlight Serenade)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS / Mutual</strong><br>1939-08-23<br>10:00 PM EDST</td>\n<td><strong>Liggett &amp; Myers</strong><br>Chesterfield Cigarettes (&quot;They Satisfy&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>Glenn Miller Orchestra</strong>, <strong>Ray Eberle</strong>, <strong>Marion Hutton</strong>, <strong>Tex Beneke</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Liggett &amp; Myers Radio Division</strong>, <strong>Paul Douglas</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Live Remote&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Glenn Miller and his Orchestra broadcast live from the Glen Island Casino on Long Island Sound, performing Miller&#39;s signature theme &#39;Moonlight Serenade&#39; alongside Ray Eberle singing &#39;Blue Evening&#39; and Marion Hutton belting out &#39;The Man with the Mandolin&#39; for Chesterfield Cigarettes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Treasury_Agent_1955_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>TCOT Desperate Diamond Dealer</code>, <code>Joe Lincoln Diamond Smugglers</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Desperate Diamond Dealer&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1955-08-23<br>8:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>U.S. Treasury Dept.</strong><br>Sustaining Radio Crime Series</td>\n<td><strong>Robert Sloane</strong> (Chief Agent Joe Lincoln), <strong>Lawson Zerbe</strong>, <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Phillips H. Lord</strong> (Creator), <strong>Leonard Valenta</strong> (Director), <strong>Carl Warren</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Police&quot;, &quot;Procedural&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;True Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Chief Treasury Agent Joe Lincoln tracks an international diamond syndicate smuggling raw industrial gems across the Canadian border inside false-bottom gasoline tankers, springing a sting operation on a debt-ridden Antwerp gem cutter in upstate New York.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Fantasy_1947_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Entity From The Void</code>, <code>Deep Space Cosmic Creature</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Entity from the Void&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mutual (WOR)</strong><br>1947-08-23<br>9:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Sustaining</strong><br>Mutual Fantasy &amp; Sci-Fi</td>\n<td><strong>Bret Morrison</strong> (Narrator), <strong>John Gibson</strong>, <strong>Gertrude Warner</strong></td>\n<td><strong>George Lowther</strong> (Writer), <strong>Jock MacGregor</strong> (Director), <strong>Jack Amerine</strong> (SFX)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Science Fiction&quot;, &quot;Horror&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>An astrophysicist operating a mountaintop observatory captures radio transmissions from deep space that materialize as a shapeless, energy-consuming cosmic entity, threatening to drain the electrical grid of the entire Eastern Seaboard.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Great_Plays_1942_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Monday Morning Class</code>, <code>NBC University of Air 1942</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Monday Morning Class (NBC University of the Air)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Blue (WJZ)</strong><br>1942-08-23<br>3:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>NBC Inter-American</strong><br>University of the Air</td>\n<td><strong>Blevins Davis</strong> (Host), <strong>Donald Crisp</strong>, <strong>Arthur Byron</strong>, <strong>Selena Royle</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Blevins Davis</strong> (Writer, Producer &amp; Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Educational&quot;, &quot;Theatre&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Host Blevins Davis presents an educational drama chronicling a dedicated Midwestern high school literature teacher who inspires a generation of working-class students during the Great Depression by introducing them to classic world dramatic masterpieces.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Gold_Coast_1949_08_23.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>George Left Out</code>, <code>AFRS Gold Coast Rhythm 1949</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;AFRS Gold Coast Rhythm Show: &#39;Big George&#39; Miller with Count Basie &amp; Dinah Washington&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Transcription</strong><br>1949-08-23<br>(Chicago / Great Lakes)</td>\n<td><strong>Non-commercial</strong><br>Armed Forces Radio Service</td>\n<td><strong>Staff Sgt. George &#39;Big George&#39; Miller</strong> (Host / DJ)</td>\n<td><strong>AFRS Chicago</strong>, <strong>Bob Carlson</strong> (Sound Engineer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Jazz&quot;, &quot;Rhythm and Blues&quot;, &quot;Military&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Disc jockey Big George Miller spins swinging jump blues, bebop, and rhythm-and-blues sides for military service personnel stationed overseas, joking about being left out of the weekend base pass list between Duke Ellington and Dinah Washington tracks.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 23</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 23</strong>: 12</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
