    "html": "<h2>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for 6 historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 22</strong> across American network and syndicated broadcasting history (1943–2010):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Inventory</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Sherlock_Holmes_2004_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Offering of the Gulls</code>, <code>Cornish Lighthouse 2004</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Further Adventures of Sherlock Holmes: The Offering of the Gulls&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Imagination Theatre</strong><br>2004-08-22<br>(XM Satellite)</td>\n<td><strong>National Syndication</strong></td>\n<td><strong>John Patrick Lowrie</strong> (Holmes), <strong>Lawrence Albert</strong> (Watson), <strong>Rick May</strong></td>\n<td><strong>M.J. Elliott</strong> (Writer), <strong>Jim French</strong> (Director), <strong>Terry Rose</strong> (Music)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Victorian&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Sherlock Holmes and Dr. Watson investigate an eerie murder at an isolated Cornish lighthouse where an eccentric watchmaker&#39;s body was discovered surrounded by circling gulls and intricately disassembled chronometers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Harry_Nile_2010_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Desperate Dealer</code>, <code>Danno O&#39;Mahony Ransom</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Danno O&#39;Mahony: Ransom &amp; Harry Nile: The Case of the Desperate Dealer&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Imagination Theatre</strong><br>2010-08-22<br>(KIXI Seattle)</td>\n<td><strong>National Syndication</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Larry Albert</strong> (Harry Nile), <strong>Pat French</strong> (Murphy), <strong>John Armstrong</strong> (Danno)</td>\n<td><strong>Jim French</strong> (Writer &amp; Director), <strong>Terry Rose</strong> (Music)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Noir&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>A syndicated radio drama double-feature pairing a high-stakes kidnapping case investigated by Dublin detective Danno O&#39;Mahony with a Seattle noir mystery where Harry Nile must protect an art collector hunted by a desperate antiquities dealer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Fitch_Bandwagon_1943_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Freddie Martin Fitch</code>, <code>Tonight We Love 1943</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Freddie Martin and his Orchestra: &#39;Tonight We Love&#39; (Fitch Bandwagon)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1943-08-22<br>7:30 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>F.W. Fitch Company</strong><br>Fitch&#39;s Dandruff Remover Shampoo</td>\n<td><strong>Freddie Martin Orchestra</strong>, <strong>Clyde Rogers</strong> (Vocalist), <strong>Freddie Martin</strong> (Sax)</td>\n<td><strong>Ward Byron</strong> (Director), <strong>Toby Reed</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Big Band&quot;, &quot;Swing&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master of Ceremonies Toby Reed welcomes Freddie Martin and his Orchestra to the Fitch Bandwagon, performing Martin&#39;s chart-topping Tchaikovsky swing adaptation &#39;Tonight We Love&#39; alongside vocal features by Clyde Rogers for Fitch Shampoo.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Light_Crust_1946_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>The Outcasts</code>, <code>Texas Quality Flour 1946</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Outcasts (Texas Western Swing with Light Crust Flour)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Texas Quality (WBAP)</strong><br>1946-08-22<br>12:30 PM CST</td>\n<td><strong>Burrus Mill &amp; Elevator</strong><br>Light Crust Flour (&quot;Best Biscuits&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>The Light Crust Doughboys</strong>, <strong>Cecil Brower</strong>, <strong>Smokey Montgomery</strong>, <strong>Parker Willson</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Burrus Mill Radio Division</strong>, <strong>Mel Gallagher</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Western&quot;, &quot;Country&quot;, &quot;Western Swing&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>The legendary Light Crust Doughboys deliver Texas western swing tunes and country humor from Fort Worth, fiddling through &#39;The Outcasts&#39; and &#39;Pappy&#39;s Old Banjo&#39; while promoting Light Crust Flour for midday homemakers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Molle_Mystery_1944_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Talking Pillow</code>, <code>Whispering Pillow Murder</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Case of the Talking Pillow&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WEAF)</strong><br>1944-08-22<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>The Centaur Co. / Sterling</strong><br>Mollé Brushless Shaving Cream</td>\n<td><strong>Bernard Lenrow</strong> (Geoffrey Barnes), <strong>Frank Lovejoy</strong>, <strong>Lesley Woods</strong>, <strong>Joseph Julian</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Frank Telford</strong> (Director), <strong>Dan Seymour</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Detective&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Crime&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Geoffrey Barnes presents the macabre murder of an insomniac pharmaceutical executive whose embroidered feather pillow was wired with an electro-acoustic speaker by his resentful partner to drive him to insanity before suffocating him.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Molle_Mystery_1947_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Man Hunt</code>, <code>Rogue Male Geoffrey Household</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Man Hunt (Adapted from Geoffrey Household&#39;s &#39;Rogue Male&#39;)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (WNBC)</strong><br>1947-08-22<br>10:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>The Centaur Co. / Sterling</strong><br>Mollé Brushless Shaving Cream</td>\n<td><strong>Bernard Lenrow</strong> (Geoffrey Barnes), <strong>Karl Swenson</strong>, <strong>Anne Seymour</strong>, <strong>Mandel Kramer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Geoffrey Household</strong> (Author), <strong>Lawrence Klee</strong> (Adapter), <strong>Frank Telford</strong> (Director)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Thriller&quot;, &quot;Suspense&quot;, &quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Geoffrey Barnes introduces an adaptation of Rogue Male, chronicling an aristocratic British big-game hunter who attempts an assassination of a European dictator, only to be hunted across the Dorset countryside by ruthless secret police assassins.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 22</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 22</strong>: 224</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
