    "html": "<h2>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Metadata Enrichment</h2>\n<p>This archival dispatch documents the comprehensive provenance, broadcast scheduling, commercial sponsorship, credited cast, production personnel, alternate circulating titles, and story synopses for 6 historical broadcasts transmitting on <strong>August 22</strong> across American network, public, and syndicated broadcasting history (1937–1984):</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>August 22 Exhaustive Archival Inventory</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename &amp; Alternate Titles</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Network, Airdate &amp; Showtime</th>\n<th>Sponsor &amp; Commercial Ads</th>\n<th>Credited Cast &amp; Performers</th>\n<th>Director, Writers &amp; Crew</th>\n<th>Genres</th>\n<th>Story-First Plot Synopsis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Radio_Readers_Digest_1943_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Corpse at Table</code>, <code>Submarine Pioneer</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Corpse at the Table &amp; Submarine Pioneer John Philip Holland&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CBS (WABC)</strong><br>1943-08-22<br>9:00 PM EWT</td>\n<td><strong>Campbell Soup Co.</strong><br>&quot;M&#39;m! M&#39;m! Good!&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>Conrad Nagel</strong> (Host / Narrator), dramatic radio ensemble</td>\n<td><strong>William Spier</strong> (Director), <strong>Lyn Murray</strong> (Music)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Anthology&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Conrad Nagel narrates a dramatic double-bill adapted from The Reader&#39;s Digest, exploring an eerie Parisian dinner party mystery involving an uninvited guest, alongside the true biographical saga of Irish-American inventor John Philip Holland pioneering the modern submarine for the U.S. Navy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Minds_Eye_1984_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Benito Sereno</code>, <code>Benito Cereno Melville</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Benito Cereno (Starring Peter Coyote &amp; Paul Winfield)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NPR / Mind&#39;s Eye</strong><br>1984-08-22<br>(Syndicated)</td>\n<td><strong>CPB / NPR</strong><br>National Public Radio</td>\n<td><strong>Peter Coyote</strong> (Capt. Amasa Delano), <strong>Paul Winfield</strong> (Babo), <strong>Tony Jay</strong> (Don Benito Cereno)</td>\n<td><strong>Herman Melville</strong> (Author), <strong>Erik Bauersfeld</strong> (Adapter), <strong>Jim McKee</strong> (Sound Design)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Drama&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;, &quot;Mystery&quot;, &quot;Literature&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>In an atmospheric adaptation of Herman Melville&#39;s novella, American merchant captain Amasa Delano boards a battered Spanish slave ship adrift off Chile, oblivious to the fact that the seemingly humble servant Babo is orchestrating an elaborate psychological deception over traumatized captain Don Benito Cereno.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Adventures_Research_1944_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Heritage Of Soil 2</code>, <code>Carver Tuskegee</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;George Washington Carver: Heritage of the Soil (Part 2)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KDKA / Westinghouse</strong><br>1944-08-22<br>(Syndicated Disc 138)</td>\n<td><strong>Westinghouse Electric</strong><br>&amp; Manufacturing Company</td>\n<td><strong>Dr. Phillips Thomas</strong> (The Research Storyteller)</td>\n<td><strong>Westinghouse Radio Division</strong>, <strong>Paul Shannon</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Educational&quot;, &quot;Science&quot;, &quot;Biography&quot;, &quot;Historical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Dr. Phillips Thomas chronicles the second part of agricultural pioneer Dr. George Washington Carver&#39;s life at Tuskegee Institute, explaining how his revolutionary crop-rotation research and hundreds of industrial peanut products revitalized Southern agriculture.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Adventures_Research_1950_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Pierre Bretonneva</code>, <code>Tracheotomy Pioneer</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Pierre Bretonneau and the First Diphtheria Tracheotomy&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KDKA / Westinghouse</strong><br>1950-08-22<br>(Syndicated)</td>\n<td><strong>Westinghouse Electric</strong><br>Corporation</td>\n<td><strong>Dr. Phillips Thomas</strong> (Storyteller)</td>\n<td><strong>Westinghouse Public Relations</strong>, <strong>Paul Shannon</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Educational&quot;, &quot;Science&quot;, &quot;Biography&quot;, &quot;Medical&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Dr. Phillips Thomas dramatizes the groundbreaking medical breakthrough of 19th-century French physician Pierre Bretonneau, who performed the first successful emergency tracheotomy in 1825 to save children suffering from lethal diphtheria asphyxiation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Magic_Key_1937_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Magic Key 1937</code>, <code>John Charles Thomas Benchley</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;John Charles Thomas and Robert Benchley with Frank Black&#39;s Orchestra&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC Blue (WJZ)</strong><br>1937-08-22<br>2:00 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Radio Corp. of America</strong><br>RCA Victor Radios &amp; Phonographs</td>\n<td><strong>Frank Black and Orchestra</strong>, <strong>John Charles Thomas</strong> (Baritone), <strong>Robert Benchley</strong> (Humorist)</td>\n<td><strong>Thomas L. Stix</strong> (Director), <strong>Milton Cross</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Classical&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;, &quot;Comedy&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Master of ceremonies Milton Cross and conductor Frank Black present an international musical showcase for RCA Victor, featuring opera baritone John Charles Thomas singing &#39;Trees&#39;, a dry comedic lecture by Robert Benchley, and transatlantic remote music pickups from London.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Rancho_Canova_1948_08_22.mp3</code> <br><em>Alt</em>: <code>Judy&#39;s Rodeo Contest</code>, <code>Rancho Canova 1948</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Judy Enters the County Fair Bronco Rodeo&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NBC (KFI)</strong><br>1948-08-22<br>9:30 PM EDT</td>\n<td><strong>Colgate-Palmolive-Peet</strong><br>Halo Shampoo &amp; Palmolive Soap</td>\n<td><strong>Judy Canova</strong>, <strong>Mel Blanc</strong> (Pedro), <strong>Ruth Perrott</strong> (Aunt Aggie), <strong>Ruby Dandridge</strong>, <strong>Sportsmen Quartet</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fred Fox &amp; Henry Hoople</strong> (Writers), <strong>Joe Rines</strong> (Director), <strong>Howard Petrie</strong> (Announcer)</td>\n<td><code>[&quot;Comedy&quot;, &quot;Sitcom&quot;, &quot;Music&quot;, &quot;Variety&quot;]</code></td>\n<td>Hillbilly comedy star Judy Canova enters a bucking bronco competition at the county fair to win prize money for Aunt Aggie&#39;s dilapidated tractor, creating chaos in the arena when Pedro mistakes the rodeo clown for a tax collector.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Archival Verification Summary</em></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Target Calendar Day</strong>: August 22</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcasts Resolved This Increment</strong>: 6</li>\n<li><strong>Cumulative Resolved on August 22</strong>: 128</li>\n<li><strong>Target Repositories Synchronized</strong>: <code>radioindex.org-meta</code> and <code>radioindex.org-blog</code></li>\n</ul>\n",
