    "html": "<h2>Batch 84 Forensic Discoveries</h2>\n<p>Batch 84 recovers and dates 15 historical broadcasts spanning classic BBC Radio literary adaptations, symphonic concert hours, country music heritage milestones, pre-Code Hollywood musical previews, foundational 1926 Chicago radio serials, and big band recruitment programs: 3 celebrated BBC Radio 4 readings of Roald Dahl macabre masterpieces on <em><strong>Roald Dahl Short Stories</strong></em> (featuring Patricia Routledge, Joanna David, and Jenny Agutter, 1990), 3 symphonic salon broadcasts conducted by Dr. Frank Black on <em><strong>The Cadillac Symphony Orchestra</strong></em> (NBC, 1951), 3 rare national radio spot announcements inaugurating the country music industry&#39;s first official celebration on <em><strong>National Country Music Week 1961</strong></em> (featuring Tex Ritter, Buck Owens, and George Hamilton IV), 2 preview chapters for Busby Berkeley&#39;s musical <em>Gold Diggers of 1935</em> on <em><strong>Direct from Hollywood</strong></em>, 2 historic May 1926 78 RPM broadcast discs from Freeman Gosden and Charles Correll on <em><strong>Sam and Henry</strong></em> (WGN Chicago), and Les Brown&#39;s Band of Renown on <em><strong>The Les Brown Show</strong></em> (1953).</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. <em>Roald Dahl Short Stories</em> &amp; <em>Cadillac Symphony Orchestra</em> (1951–1990)</h2>\n<p>Literary suspense and lush classical-popular orchestra arrangements:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Verified Airdate</th>\n<th>Performers &amp; Repertoire</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Roald_Dahl_Heaven_Routledge.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Roald Dahl Short Stories: &#39;The Way Up to Heaven&#39; (read by Patricia Routledge)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1990-09-17</strong></td>\n<td>Patricia Routledge masterfully portraying the pathological fear of missed planes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Roald_Dahl_Edward_David.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Roald Dahl Short Stories: &#39;Edward the Conqueror&#39; (read by Joanna David)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1990-09-24</strong></td>\n<td>A strange stray cat with an eerie affinity for Liszt and Chopin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Roald_Dahl_Lamb_Agutter.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Roald Dahl Short Stories: &#39;Lamb to the Slaughter&#39; (read by Jenny Agutter)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1990-10-01</strong></td>\n<td>The quintessential frozen leg of lamb murder weapon.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Cadillac_Sym_Kiss_Shadow.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Cadillac Symphony Orchestra: &#39;We Kiss in a Shadow&#39; (Dr. Frank Black)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1951-10-14</strong></td>\n<td>Rodgers &amp; Hammerstein&#39;s <em>The King and I</em> overture arranged for symphony orchestra.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Cadillac_Sym_Drink_Eyes.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Cadillac Symphony Orchestra: &#39;Drink to Me Only with Thine Eyes&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1951-10-21</strong></td>\n<td>Ben Jonson&#39;s timeless English lyric in a grand orchestral setting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Cadillac_Sym_Beautiful_Mornin.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Cadillac Symphony Orchestra: &#39;Oh, What a Beautiful Mornin&#39;&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1951-10-28</strong></td>\n<td>Uplifting American standards presented under General Motors sponsorship.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. <em>Country Music Week 1961</em>, <em>Sam and Henry</em>, &amp; <em>Les Brown</em></h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Original Circulating Filename</th>\n<th>Canonical Title</th>\n<th>Verified Airdate</th>\n<th>Historical Significance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Country_Week_George_Hamilton.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;National Country Music Week 1961: George Hamilton IV Special Promo&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1961-10-29</strong></td>\n<td>Introducing the Country Music Association&#39;s first national promotional week.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Country_Week_Tex_Ritter.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;National Country Music Week 1961: Tex Ritter Special Promo&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1961-10-30</strong></td>\n<td>Country Music Hall of Famer Tex Ritter urging fans to support local radio stations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Country_Week_Buck_Owens.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;National Country Music Week 1961: Buck Owens Special Promo&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1961-10-31</strong></td>\n<td>The pioneer of the Bakersfield Sound celebrating country radio disc jockeys.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Direct_Hollywood_Gold_Diggers_1.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Direct from Hollywood: Warner Bros. Presents &#39;Gold Diggers of 1935&#39; (Part 1)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1935-03-12</strong></td>\n<td>Dick Powell and Gloria Stuart introducing Harry Warren and Al Dubin songs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Direct_Hollywood_Gold_Diggers_2.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Direct from Hollywood: Warner Bros. Presents &#39;Gold Diggers of 1935&#39; (Part 2)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1935-03-19</strong></td>\n<td>Behind the scenes of Busby Berkeley&#39;s &#39;Lullaby of Broadway&#39;.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Sam_Henry_Meadow_Lark.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Sam and Henry: &#39;The Meadow Lark Song &amp; Chicago Dreams&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1926-05-15</strong></td>\n<td>Gosden and Correll&#39;s earliest radio serial characters in Chicago.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Sam_Henry_Lodge_Speech.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;Sam and Henry: &#39;Sam&#39;s Speech at the Lodge Hall&#39;&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1926-05-27</strong></td>\n<td>Rare Victor electrical recording preserving early live network ancestor radio.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Les_Brown_Bizet.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Les Brown Show: &#39;Bizet Has His Day&#39; (Band of Renown)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1953-06-16</strong></td>\n<td>Classical swing adaptation featuring Les Brown&#39;s precision horn arrangements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Les_Brown_How_About_You.mp3</code></td>\n<td><strong>&quot;The Les Brown Show: &#39;How About You&#39; (US Navy Recruitment)&quot;</strong></td>\n<td><strong>1953-06-23</strong></td>\n<td>Upbeat big band standard supporting US Naval reserve enlistment.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n",
